---
// ===========================================================================
// paths.astro — how a path gets a type, and why it took a registry.
//
// The page exists because the answer is counter-intuitive: the types do not
// travel with the form. Written as the problem first, since the design only
// reads as reasonable once the constraint that forced it is on the page.
// ===========================================================================
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TypeAndInstance from "../components/TypeAndInstance.astro";

const registration = `
// src/form-registry.ts — one file, one declaration, one time.
const orderAdapter = zodFormResolver(orderSchema);

declare module "form-react" {
  interface FormTypeRegistry {
    form: typeof orderAdapter;
  }
}
`;

const usage = `
// Any component, at any depth. It imports nothing from the file above.
const postcode = useField("billing.postcode");  // string | undefined, inferred
const typo     = useField("billing.postcod");   // compile error
const sku      = useField("items[0].sku");      // a place, checked as its rule
const computed = useField(\`items[\${i}].sku\`);   // a computed index is fine
const spliced  = useField(\`items[\${s}].sku\`);   // compile error: s is a string
`;

const rows = `
// A row hands down its own address, and the address carries a type:
// \`row.path\` is \`shipments[\${number}]\`, not \`string\`.
{shipments.rows.map((shipment) => (
  <Lines key={shipment.key} at={shipment.path} />
))}

function Lines({ at }: { at: \`shipments[\${number}]\` }) {
  const lines = useRows(\`\${at}.lines\`);          // checked
  return lines.rows.map((line) => (
    <Text key={line.key} at={\`\${line.path}.sku\`} />   // checked, three levels down
  ));
}
`;

const several = `
declare module "form-react" {
  interface FormTypeRegistry {
    order: typeof orderAdapter;
    profile: typeof profileAdapter;
  }
}

useField("order", "items[0].sku");
useField("profile", "items[0].sku");  // compile error: profile has no items
useField("items[0].sku");             // no key: checked against every form
`;

const dynamic = `
// A schema built from a response, or generated by a benchmark harness.
declare module "form-react" {
  interface FormTypeRegistry {
    form: FormAdapter<unknown, string>;
  }
}
`;

const viewTypes = [
  [
    "FormPath",
    "Every registered path, containers included.",
    "A widget that takes whatever it is handed.",
  ],
  [
    "FormPathTo<string>",
    "Only the paths whose value is a string.",
    "A text input. Hand it a number field and it does not compile.",
  ],
  [
    "FormPathOver<'postcode' | 'city'>",
    "Only the prefixes that have all of those underneath.",
    "An address section placed at both billing and shipping.",
  ],
];
---

<Base
  title="Typed paths — form-contract"
  description="One React context serves every form, so the context cannot be generic and a path union cannot travel through it. The types are registered once by the application instead, and a component ten levels down is checked without being handed anything."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold sm:text-4xl">Typed paths</h1>

  <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">A misspelt field path is a compile error
    instead of an input that renders blank and is never validated</strong> —
    inside arrays, and with a computed index. A typo in an optional-chained
    error lookup is worse than blank: it reads as <em>no error here</em>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">One declaration, once</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    One file registers the types for the whole application, and a component ten
    levels down is checked without being handed anything.
  </p>

  <Code code={registration} />
  <Code code={usage} lang="tsx" />

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Skip it and the hooks are not unchecked — they are refused, and the error
    quotes the declaration you are missing. A module augmentation belongs to
    the <em>compilation</em>, not to the import graph, which is why a component
    that never imports the registry is still checked by it, and why a
    registration file dropping out of <code>tsconfig</code>'s{" "}
    <code>include</code> fails loudly rather than degrading to{" "}
    <code>string</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Why a registry and not a prop</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Because the types cannot travel with the instance: one React context object
    serves every form in an application, so the context cannot be generic and a
    path union cannot pass through it.
  </p>

  <TypeAndInstance />

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Interpolated paths stay checked</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    A path assembled three levels inside a list is still checked, and an index
    that arrived as a string does not compile. Checking that stopped at the
    first array would be worth little: a nested form is where paths are
    actually built by hand.
  </p>

  <Code code={rows} lang="tsx" />

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    A number index is accepted and a string one is not, because a template
    literal type can tell them apart — and a mis-built path is almost always a
    string that got concatenated in.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Several forms are several keys</h2>

  <Code code={several} />

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    The key names a form in the <em>registry</em>; the instance still comes
    from the nearest provider, and nothing relates the two. So the provider
    carries its key —{" "}
    <code>&lt;FormProvider form={"{form}"} formKey="order"&gt;</code> — and a
    keyed hook throws when they disagree.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">Path types for the view layer</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">
    A widget receives a path rather than knowing one. These say which ones it
    can take.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead class="text-left text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="hairline">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Type</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">What it is</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-medium">Where it goes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          viewTypes.map(([name, what, where]) => (
            <tr class="hairline">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">{name}</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 align-top leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
                {what}
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 align-top leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
                {where}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    All three are computed from the registered schema rather than listed, so a
    third address arriving in it needs no edit in the section that draws one.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">A schema nobody wrote down</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-on-surface-variant">
    Built from a response, or generated by a benchmark. It registers{" "}
    <code>string</code>.
  </p>

  <Code code={dynamic} />

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    <code>AddressablePath&lt;string&gt;</code> is <code>string</code>, so that
    form is back to unchecked paths — by its own declaration, in one place a
    reader can find. The escape is a line of the application's own source rather
    than a second API everyone else has to choose between.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl font-bold">At run time none of this exists</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    Whether a path is real is the <em>store's</em> question, and the store
    answers it for every caller — every hook, every <code>&lt;Field&gt;</code>{" "}
    and every non-React consumer funnels through <code>form.field()</code>.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6 overflow-hidden rounded-lg bg-surface-low p-5">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed break-words">
      [form-contract] "billing.postcod" is not a field this form has, so it will
      draw nothing and validate nothing. Did you mean: billing.postcode,
      billing, billing.city?
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-on-surface-variant">
    It <strong class="text-on-surface">warns, once per path; it does not
    throw</strong>. A mis-addressed field is inert, but it cannot let bad data
    through: the pass judges the whole root, so the verdict and the submit gate
    stay correct and what actually broke is one field's display. Taking the
    whole form down for that is not a trade worth making.
  </p>
</Base>
